// Exercise 5.2 - one leaf stops being taken on faith.
//
// ../bricks/graceful.jd says "The test dataset is trustworthy" and stops there, as a leaf.
// That is a large thing to assert without argument. ../bricks/trustworthy.jd is the
// argument for it, written separately by whoever cared about the dataset.
//
// Check your work with:   jpipe diagnostic -i 02_refine.jd

load "../bricks/graceful.jd"

// TODO 1. load ../bricks/trustworthy.jd, beside the load above.
//
//         Type `load "` and press ⌃Space if you would rather pick the path than type it.

// TODO 2. write a justification named `graceful_deep` that refines `graceful` with
//         `trustworthy`:
//
//         justification <name> is refine(<base>, <detail>) {
//             hook: "<element id>"
//         }
//
//         refine takes exactly two models, and the order is the meaning: the base first,
//         the argument being grafted in second.
//
//         Inside the quotes after `hook:`, press ⌃Space. The editor lists the evidence
//         leaves of the base, each with its label beside it, because a leaf is the only
//         thing a refinement can replace. Pick the one about the test dataset.
//
//         The result needs a name of its own. A model cannot refine itself into its own
//         name, and the compiler says so plainly if you try.

